pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.slough.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Slough Council faces \u00a319.9m overspend with government-appointed commissioners warning of financial collapse by year-end.","lead":"Slough Council's Cabinet heard on 21 September that it is heading for a \u00a319.9m overspend in 2026-27, driven largely by \u00a315.955m of unforeseen costs in children's social care placements. Government-appointed commissioners overseeing the council declared the position \"extremely serious\" and warned that without \"structured and swift intervention\" the authority faces financial collapse before the financial year closes. The overspend will deplete the General Fund reserve, forecast at \u00a322.313m before the deficit, if in-year savings cannot fully offset it. Children's social care spending has risen 26 per cent in placement numbers and 40 per cent in unit costs, reflecting a small cohort of approximately 15 children in placements costing more than \u00a3500,000 per year each. The commissioners required visible leadership from elected members and senior officers and asked that non-statutory projects be deferred to demonstrate commitment to financial recovery.","iso":"2026-09-21","paper_title":"Housing Benefit War Pension Disregard Policy Report","paper_url":"https://slough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s89326/89326.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Slough faces \u00a319.9m overspend as children's social care costs surge and government commissioners warn of collapse risk","lead":"Slough Council is forecast to overspend its budget by \u00a319.9m in 2026\u201327, a position government-appointed commissioners have described as \"extremely serious\" and requiring \"swift intervention\" to avoid financial collapse by year-end. Children's social care accounts for \u00a315.955m of the deficit, driven by a 26% jump in placement numbers and 40% growth in costs, with approximately 15 children in placements costing more than \u00a3500,000 each annually. The council is also facing a dedicated schools grant deficit that could reach \u00a354.8m by March 2028 if government support lapses, and a \u00a327.7m asset disposal programme of which only \u00a33m has been received. General Fund reserves stand at \u00a322.313m but face severe depletion if in-year mitigations fail to offset the overspend. The commissioners have ordered elected members and senior officers to exercise \"voluntary restraint on political priorities\" and defer non-statutory projects to signal seriousness.","iso":"2026-09-21","paper_title":"Housing Benefit War Pension Disregard Policy Report","paper_url":"https://slough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s89326/89326.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Slough braces for financial collapse as children's care costs spiral \u00a316m over budget","lead":"Slough council faces a general fund overspend of \u00a319.9m in the current financial year, with government-appointed commissioners declaring the position \"extremely serious\" and requiring \"structured and swift intervention\" to avoid financial collapse by year-end. Children's social care accounts for \u00a315.955m of the shortfall \u2014 80 per cent of the total deficit \u2014 driven by a 26 per cent rise in the number of children in placements and a 40 per cent jump in average costs per child. About 15 children in specialist placements each cost more than \u00a3500,000 a year. The council is drawing on reserves and racing to sell assets to plug the gap, but has received only \u00a33m of a planned \u00a327.7m in property sales so far. Commissioners have demanded visible leadership from elected members and a willingness to defer or pause non-statutory projects to signal seriousness about recovery.","iso":"2026-09-21","paper_title":"Housing Benefit War Pension Disregard Policy Report","paper_url":"https://slough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s89326/89326.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit and Corporate Governance Committee","paper_title":"Slough Council Audit progress report and sector update September 2026","paper_url":"https://slough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s89501/89501.pdf","headline":"Value for Money findings on financial sustainability and governance are complete but not yet disclosed.","detail":"The auditors have completed their assessment of whether the council has made proper arrangements for financial sustainability, governance, and value for money under the Code of Audit Practice. Findings are pending management responses and will be published in the Auditor's Annual Report in November. The substance and any material concerns are not yet public.","category":"audit","matters":"VFM findings on financial sustainability and governance assess the council's financial health and control environment\u2014information material to lender credit decisions.","figures":"Results scheduled for November 2026 Audit Committee meeting.","quote":"Our work is complete and at the time of writing this report we are awaiting management's responses to the findings. Our findings will be set out in our Auditor's Annual Report, and this will be presented at the November Audit and Corporate Governance Committee","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit and Corporate Governance Committee","paper_title":"Slough Council Audit progress report and sector update September 2026","paper_url":"https://slough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s89501/89501.pdf","headline":"It is unlikely that a full in-year audit will be completed for 2025/26.","detail":"The auditors explicitly state that due to large sample sizes driven by historical issues and data quality problems, a full in-year audit will not be completed. The focus is instead limited to gaining assurance on in-year revenue transactions and certain year-end balances only.","category":"audit","matters":"An incomplete audit scope results in a qualified or limited audit opinion, reducing the assurance available to lenders about the accuracy and completeness of the financial statements.","figures":"No specific figures; audit limitation stated in progress summary for 2025/26.","quote":"Due to the matters above it is unlikely that a full in-year audit will be completed for 2025/26.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit and Corporate Governance Committee","paper_title":"Slough Council Audit progress report and sector update September 2026","paper_url":"https://slough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s89501/89501.pdf","headline":"Debtor and creditor balances lack clean transaction data, driving abnormally large sample sizes and delaying audit completion.","detail":"The council's financial system derives debtor and creditor lists by rolling forward prior balances and adjusting for in-year transactions. This method makes it impossible to identify which items are actual year-end balances, since tested items may have already been paid or received. The council has attempted cleansing but substantial duplicate and contradictory entries remain. The auditors recommend full transaction listing cleansing before next year's audit.","category":"audit","matters":"Unreliable financial data in the accounts production process indicates control weaknesses that increase audit risk and governance concerns for lenders assessing financial systems integrity.","figures":"948 total sample items selected; 549 (58%) remain outstanding as at report date. Creditors testing: 31 outstanding; Payments made: 23 outstanding.","quote":"Break downs provided, from the financial system, to support these balances were derived from a balance bought forward adjusted for in year receipts and payments. This makes selecting samples for testing difficult as items tested could have been paid or received and are therefore no longer year end balances.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit and Corporate Governance Committee","paper_title":"Annual Governance Statement Q1 Update Report","paper_url":"https://slough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s89536/89536.pdf","headline":"Statutory compliance concerns on FOI and SAR with limited capacity to improve","detail":"Information governance rated 2 red, 1 amber. Council faces concerns about compliance with statutory FOI and subject access request (SAR) requirements, with limited organisational capacity to pursue strategic improvements. Issue links to Local Government Transparency Code compliance.","category":"governance","matters":"Statutory non-compliance is a governance red flag; councils in serious financial trouble often display weak information governance and transparency alongside financial failure.","figures":"2 red, 1 amber rating in information governance governance","quote":"There remains concern about compliance with statutory requirements for FOI and SAR and there is limited organisational capacity to consider more strategic options to improve compliance.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Audit and Corporate Governance Committee","paper_title":"Annual Governance Statement Q1 Update Report","paper_url":"https://slough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s89536/89536.pdf","headline":"External review commissioned on Slough Children First before making in-year and future funding decisions","detail":"Slough Children First (council-owned company delivering children's services) carries ongoing governance risks rated 1 red, despite recent board-level improvements. The council has commissioned external review of performance and financial efficiency before committing further in-year or future funding.","category":"other","matters":"External review before funding commitment signals material performance or financial concerns; likely indicates substantial continued subsidy requirement.","figures":"1 red rating for Slough Children First within children's services governance","quote":"The Council has also commissioned external support to review performance and financial efficiency before making final decisions on in-year and future year funding.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-21"},"name":"Slough UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-23","since_iso":"2026-08-24","distress":true,"headline":"Council has 1,500+ households in temporary accommodation, mostly in expensive nightly-let provision","lead":"Shows the scale and cost of the temporary accommodation challenge. A lender would want to understand that the council is facing substantial homelessness demand and associated service costs that justify this capital investment.","also":["13 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Corporate Improvement Scrutiny Committee on 2026-09-29","n_findings":14,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"HRA Acquisitions - Purchase of Moor Furlong and Eltham Avenue Report","top_paper_url":"https://slough.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s89428/89428.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":195,"news_drafts_total":3}}